# Timer: the fleet drain — nightly 01:07 America/Los_Angeles (dotfiles-69qr).
#
# ⚠️ NOT INSTALLED, NOT ENABLED. The maiden run is SUPERVISED and hand-launched
# post-cutover (Zig's OQ1 resolution); see the launch line in
# pulse-marshal.service. This file exists so the schedule is REVIEWABLE before
# it is live, and so installing it later is a copy, not a design decision made
# at install time.
#
# 01:07 AND NOT 01:00. Round minutes are where every hand-written timer lands,
# and a pileup on the same second means several ticks contend for the tmux
# server and the same subscription at once. The drain's window is 01:00-06:00
# PT (OQ1), so it starts seven minutes inside it — late enough to be clear of
# anything on the hour, early enough to leave the full five hours. The
# neighbours on this box are 04:13 (dream, Sundays) and 06:44 (the seneschal
# brief); the drain ENDS before the brief starts, deliberately: the brief's
# DRAIN section is how Zig reviews the night, and it must read a finished
# ledger, not a live one.
#
# ⚠️ Persistent=FALSE, and this is the one row where that is right. Every other
# pulse unit sets Persistent=true because a missed brief or digest is worth
# catching up once. A missed DRAIN is not: catch-up fires at boot or at
# whatever hour the machine came back, which is precisely the daytime the
# budget's zig_daytime_reserve exists to protect — builders swarming through
# Zig's own working hours, spending the tap he is trying to use. A missed night
# is a night the queue simply waits; the next 01:07 drains it.
#
# ⚠️ TIMER-RENAME / FIRST-ENABLE GOTCHA (~/.agents/infra.md). systemd's
# persistence state lives in ~/.local/share/systemd/timers/stamp-<unit>.timer,
# NOT in the unit file — a unit name that has never run has no stamp. With
# Persistent=false that cannot fire an immediate catch-up, but touch the stamp
# anyway before the first enable so the unit's history starts clean:
#
#   touch ~/.local/share/systemd/timers/stamp-pulse-marshal.timer
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now pulse-marshal.timer
#   systemctl --user list-timers | grep marshal    # LAST column empty/past
[Unit]
Description=Pulse timer: nightly fleet drain at 01:07 PT (SUPERVISED maiden run first)

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 01:07:00 America/Los_Angeles
Persistent=false

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
